{"audio":{"src":"https://pub-13abeb101e2945d2a7f60234f6faf52e.r2.dev/materials/a2-spain-part-1/audio/main-dbf8e227ffdc.mp3","durationSec":285.672,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":2.74,"text":"Most people in Spain live in flats, not houses."},{"start":3.4,"end":6.34,"text":"Their homes are often small, and the weather is often warm."},{"start":7.04,"end":8.94,"text":"So a lot of life happens outside."},{"start":9.74,"end":12.34,"text":"People meet friends in cafés and in the plaza."},{"start":13.18,"end":15.98,"text":"The plaza is the big square in the middle of a town."},{"start":16.54,"end":18.74,"text":"It is like a big living room for everyone."},{"start":19.92,"end":22.18,"text":"In summer, the afternoons are very hot."},{"start":22.64,"end":25.1,"text":"So people stay inside in the hottest hours."},{"start":26.2,"end":28.38,"text":"In the evening, the air gets cooler."},{"start":29.08,"end":32.58,"text":"So they go out for the paseo, a slow evening walk."},{"start":32.58,"end":36.4,"text":"Families walk through the town and say hello to neighbours."},{"start":36.4,"end":38.08,"text":"The paseo is not for sport."},{"start":38.42,"end":40.6,"text":"It is a way to see your town and your friends."},{"start":42.66,"end":45.68,"text":"Before 1492, Spain was not one country."},{"start":46.04,"end":47.12,"text":"It had several kingdoms."},{"start":47.92,"end":50.34,"text":"A kingdom is a country with a king or a queen."},{"start":51.18,"end":53.84,"text":"The biggest were Castile and Aragon."},{"start":54.4,"end":58.24,"text":"In the south was Granada, the last kingdom with Muslim rulers."},{"start":58.78,"end":63.42,"text":"Isabella was the future queen of Castile, and Ferdinand was the future king of Aragon."},{"start":64.4,"end":66.72,"text":"They married, so their kingdoms worked together."},{"start":67.52,"end":70.66,"text":"Isabella and Ferdinand are called the Catholic Monarchs."},{"start":71.1,"end":73.86,"text":"Monarch is another word for king or queen."},{"start":74.6,"end":76.68,"text":"In 1492, they took Granada."},{"start":77.46,"end":79.46,"text":"Now they ruled almost all of Spain."},{"start":80.1,"end":83.1,"text":"In the same year, they paid for the ships of a sailor called Columbus."},{"start":83.62,"end":88.1,"text":"He sailed west, and on 12 October 1492, he arrived in America."},{"start":88.98,"end":92.22,"text":"That is why 12 October is the national day of Spain."},{"start":93.64,"end":96.8,"text":"Spain has 17 regions, called autonomous communities."},{"start":97.5,"end":99,"text":"Each one has its own government."},{"start":99.6,"end":102.72,"text":"It decides many things for itself, like schools and hospitals."},{"start":103.44,"end":103.68,"text":"Why?"},{"start":104.4,"end":106.62,"text":"Long ago, Spain had several kingdoms."},{"start":107.38,"end":109.64,"text":"That is why the regions are still different today."},{"start":110.12,"end":112.26,"text":"Some even have their own language."},{"start":112.26,"end":115.44,"text":"Spanish is spoken all over Spain."},{"start":116.14,"end":120.76,"text":"People there often call it castellano, because it first came from Castile."},{"start":121.46,"end":125.32,"text":"Around Barcelona, in the north-east, people also speak Catalan."},{"start":125.84,"end":129.22,"text":"In the Basque Country, in the north, people also speak Basque."},{"start":130.18,"end":132.72,"text":"Most languages belong to a language family."},{"start":133.38,"end":136.84,"text":"It is a group of languages that grew from one old language."},{"start":137.29,"end":141.24,"text":"For example, Spanish and Catalan both grew from Latin."},{"start":141.8,"end":144.78,"text":"But Basque is not in the same family as any other language."},{"start":145.32,"end":148.86,"text":"So people who speak only Spanish cannot understand it."},{"start":149.74,"end":151.64,"text":"In Granada, you can see the Alhambra."},{"start":152.12,"end":154.02,"text":"It was the palace of the Muslim kings there."},{"start":154.56,"end":156.26,"text":"The name is Arabic for the red one."},{"start":156.82,"end":157.08,"text":"Why?"},{"start":157.82,"end":159.34,"text":"The walls are made of red earth."},{"start":160.66,"end":165.08,"text":"In Barcelona, there is the Sagrada Família, a very big church by Gaudí."},{"start":165.08,"end":169.44,"text":"Building started in 1882, and it is still not finished."},{"start":170.36,"end":171.3,"text":"Why is it so slow?"},{"start":172.02,"end":173.96,"text":"The church gets no money from the government."},{"start":173.96,"end":176.88,"text":"Tickets and gifts from visitors pay for the work."},{"start":177.32,"end":179.42,"text":"So it can only grow when there is money."},{"start":179.42,"end":184.18,"text":"Lunch is the biggest meal of the day, and dinner comes late."},{"start":184.86,"end":189,"text":"These are the usual times:\n\nLunch: about 2 pm."},{"start":189,"end":191.48,"text":"Dinner: 9 pm or later."},{"start":192.42,"end":193.14,"text":"Why so late?"},{"start":193.72,"end":195.3,"text":"One reason is the clock."},{"start":196.02,"end":200.06,"text":"In 1940, the dictator Franco moved the clocks one hour forward."},{"start":200.54,"end":203.06,"text":"A dictator is a ruler with all the power."},{"start":203.82,"end":205.12,"text":"The clocks never went back."},{"start":205.86,"end":207.48,"text":"So Spain's clocks are ahead of the sun."},{"start":208.22,"end":210.26,"text":"In winter, they are about one hour ahead."},{"start":210.9,"end":213.8,"text":"So dinner at 9 pm is like 8 pm by the sun."},{"start":214.36,"end":215.24,"text":"Lunch is also long."},{"start":215.58,"end":215.8,"text":"Why?"},{"start":216.24,"end":218.48,"text":"It is the biggest meal, so people eat it slowly."},{"start":219.32,"end":221.82,"text":"After the meal, people stay at the table and talk."},{"start":222.22,"end":225.66,"text":"There is a word for this time: sobremesa, or over the table."},{"start":226.68,"end":229.32,"text":"A famous lunch dish is paella, from Valencia."},{"start":229.96,"end":232.06,"text":"It is rice cooked in a wide, flat pan."},{"start":232.7,"end":235.36,"text":"In Valencia, people call this pan a paella."},{"start":235.76,"end":237.78,"text":"So the dish has the same name as the pan."},{"start":238.8,"end":241.58,"text":"Every spring, Seville has its big fair."},{"start":241.58,"end":244.56,"text":"It started as a market for buying and selling farm animals."},{"start":245.4,"end":249.34,"text":"Today it is like a small town of colourful tents along long streets."},{"start":250.01,"end":252.08,"text":"Each tent is called a caseta."},{"start":253.02,"end":256.98,"text":"Most of them belong to families, groups of friends, clubs or companies."},{"start":257.64,"end":260.64,"text":"So you usually go in only when the owners invite you."},{"start":261.1,"end":267.16,"text":"Inside, people eat, drink and dance sevillanas, a happy dance for couples."},{"start":267.16,"end":271.42,"text":"Seville is in Andalusia, the home of flamenco."},{"start":271.84,"end":275.06,"text":"Flamenco is singing, guitar music and dance together."},{"start":275.54,"end":278.24,"text":"The dancer often hits the floor hard with the shoes."},{"start":278.82,"end":280.62,"text":"So the shoes make music too."},{"start":281.52,"end":285.22,"text":"The Romani people of Andalusia helped to create flamenco."}],"words":[{"text":"Most","start":0,"end":0.24},{"text":"people","start":0.24,"end":0.5},{"text":"in","start":0.5,"end":0.7},{"text":"Spain","start":0.7,"end":1},{"text":"live","start":1,"end":1.24},{"text":"in","start":1.24,"end":1.44},{"text":"flats","start":1.44,"end":1.86},{"text":"not","start":2.18,"end":2.28},{"text":"houses","start":2.28,"end":2.74},{"text":"Their","start":3.4,"end":3.5},{"text":"homes","start":3.5,"end":3.76},{"text":"are","start":3.76,"end":3.94},{"text":"often","start":3.94,"end":4.2},{"text":"small","start":4.2,"end":4.66},{"text":"and","start":5.06,"end":5.16},{"text":"the","start":5.16,"end":5.28},{"text":"weathe